Insomnia
Palliative & End of Life
Difficulty initiating sleep, Frequent nocturnal awakenings, Unrefreshing sleep

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PEL-036
Reason for Visit: Acute crisis of severe sleep deprivation leading to extreme daytime somnolence, falls risk, and severe existential dread preventing rest.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, primary caregiver
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported the patient has not slept for more than 45 minutes continuously over the last 72 hours, resulting in acute, severe psychological distress, hallucinations of sleep deprivation, and a near-fall today due to profound daytime somnolence. Urgent assessment was strictly necessary to rule out acute physiological drivers of the insomnia, break the cycle of autonomic hyperarousal, and provide immediate pharmacological intervention to allow the patient to sleep and prevent further functional collapse prior to the next regular in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I am losing my mind. Every time I close my eyes, the panic starts, and I just lie there in the dark waiting for morning."
History of Present Illness: Over the past month, the patient has experienced profound, persistent difficulty explicitly initiating sleep despite severe, generalized fatigue. This has escalated over the last three days into frequent, prolonged, and highly distressing nocturnal awakenings interrupting all sleep architecture. The patient reports early morning awakenings distinctly characterized by an absolute inability to explicitly return to sleep, resulting in unrefreshing sleep explicitly failing to provide any subjective sense of rest or restored energy. Severe daytime somnolence directly secondary to the profound nocturnal sleep deprivation caused a near-miss fall today. The caregiver notes heightened nocturnal anxiety, existential dread, and severe racing thoughts explicitly preventing relaxation. There is an exacerbation of underlying chronic pain and untreated nausea strictly at night. The patient exhibits a reversal of the strictly normal circadian rhythm leading to distinct daytime sleeping and nighttime wakefulness.
Functional Status: Ambulatory with assistance, but acutely impaired by profound sleep deprivation.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of falls; caregiver exhaustion from continuous nocturnal monitoring and shared sleep deprivation.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/90 mmHg | HR: 110 bpm | Temp: 36.6 C | RR: 20 bpm | SpO2: 97% on room air | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Patient appears disheveled, with pronounced periorbital dark circles, frequent yawning, and a flat, exhausted affect.
Physical Exam: Neurological exam reveals sluggish but intact cognitive processing and a fine postural tremor consistent with severe sleep deprivation. No focal neurological deficits or signs of acute delirium. Cardiovascular and respiratory exams are unremarkable for acute decompensation, ruling out orthopnea or paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea as primary drivers. The diagnostic tetrad is fulfilled by the explicit difficulty initiating sleep, the reported frequent nocturnal awakenings, and the distinctly unrefreshing sleep leading to the current state of exhaustion.
Environmental Observations: The bedroom is cluttered. The patient has been using a bright electronic tablet in bed to distract from the existential dread.
Medication Reconciliation: The patient is taking short-acting opioids for pain, which are wearing off at 0300, directly triggering nocturnal awakenings.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Insomnia - Status: Exacerbated (Acute Sleep Deprivation Crisis)
Differentials:

Hyperactive delirium - Ruled out/unlikely as the patient remains fully oriented, logical (though slow), and lacks the acute, fluctuating severe inattention or true visual hallucinations characteristic of delirium.

Uncontrolled nociceptive pain - Ruled out/unlikely as the sole driver of sleep initiation failure, as the patient explicitly identifies racing thoughts and existential dread as the primary barrier at bedtime, though pain does contribute to the early morning awakenings.

Obstructive Sleep Apnea - Ruled out/unlikely as the caregiver denies loud snoring or witnessed apneic pauses; the awakenings are driven by anxiety and pain, not airway collapse.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is in a state of critical physical and psychological exhaustion due to severe, compounded palliative insomnia. The objective findings of tachycardia, tremor, and profound somnolence indicate a nervous system locked in a state of hyperarousal despite extreme physical fatigue. Because the sleep deprivation is actively precipitating near-falls and causing intolerable existential suffering, delaying assessment until standard hours would subject the patient to another torturous night and a high risk of traumatic injury. Immediate after-hours medical intervention was essential to address the inadequate nocturnal analgesia, provide acute pharmacological sedation to force a sleep cycle tonight, and implement strict behavioral sleep hygiene.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered stat sublingual lorazepam to rapidly blunt the acute nocturnal anxiety and facilitate sleep onset tonight. Adjusted the baseline analgesic regimen, switching the evening dose to a long-acting sustained-release opioid to prevent the 0300 pain-induced awakenings.
Positive Behavioral Change: When you enter the bedroom at night (trigger), explicitly leave all electronic devices, including phones and tablets, in the kitchen (behavior). This removes the artificial blue light that suppresses your natural sleep hormones, allowing your brain to correctly recognize that it is time to shut down and rest (positive consequence).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to the community palliative care psychologist for targeted cognitive-behavioral therapy for insomnia (CBT-I) and existential distress.
DME/Safety (Optional): Ensure a dim nightlight is installed in the hallway to prevent falls during any unavoidable nighttime bathroom trips.
Follow-up: GP review in 48 hours to assess the efficacy of the modified nocturnal analgesic and sedative regimen.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the patient becomes completely unarousable the following morning, experiences a fall with head strike during the night, or develops new, severe confusion or visual hallucinations, the caregiver must call 000 or the after-hours medical service immediately.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The medication provided tonight is a temporary chemical bridge to break the cycle of exhaustion; long-term resolution requires strict adherence to the new pain regimen and sleep hygiene rules.
Clinical Course: Insomnia in the palliative phase is often multifactorial and fluctuates; consistent routines are the strongest defense against recurrence.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Caregiver: Supervise all ambulation tonight, as the new sedative will increase the risk of unsteadiness. Do not allow the patient to sleep continuously throughout the day tomorrow.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your brain is stuck in a state of "fight or flight" due to the stress of your illness, and your pain medication has been wearing off too early. The tablet tonight will act as a chemical brake to stop the racing thoughts and force your brain to sleep. The new, long-acting pain pill will ensure you stay asleep until morning.
Actionable Steps: Implement a strict "wind-down" routine 60 minutes before bed, involving zero screens. If you wake up and cannot fall back asleep within 20 minutes, leave the bed, sit in a dim room, and read a physical book until you feel sleepy again.

